WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
Our three polynomials
c, d, and g have product

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:10.140
with their star equal to nine in
the guiding case u equals three.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:15.770
At one and omega, their values
lie on a circle of radius three.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:21.040
We want to show that all three share
one rotation between these inputs.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.080
Our drawn points are an illustration,

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:28.680
not values from an order thirty
six circulant Hadamard matrix.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:34.320
Recall delta of f: f of
one divided by f of omega.

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:39.520
The delta theorem makes all three
deltas roots of unity of odd order.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.740
Compare d's phase change with c's by
R equal to delta of d over delta of c.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.860
Define W similarly using g. These
comparisons also have odd order.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.820
Here their candidates are
one, omega, and omega squared.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:00.600
The common signs give extra
information: two divides d minus c,

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.380
and one plus i divides g minus
c, coefficient by coefficient.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:08.340
Both differences therefore
vanish modulo one plus i.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:12.840
This is arithmetic, not a small
angle. Before taking ratios,

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:17.020
check the denominators. At
either input, one or omega,

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:21.030
the inverse of c is its complex
conjugate divided by nine.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:26.220
The same holds for d and g. We invert
the odd number nine before reducing;

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:30.860
its residue is one. Thus all
these evaluations remain units.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:35.100
Dividing by c now gives d over c and g

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.000
over c each congruent
to one at both inputs.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.600
R is the ratio of the two d over c values;

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.060
W is the ratio of the two g over c values.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.550
Consequently R and W
both have residue one.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:54.390
Odd order and residue one now
meet. If omega reduced to one,

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:59.530
its vanishing sum, one plus omega plus
omega squared, would become three ones.

00:01:59.805 --> 00:02:03.770
Two cancel in characteristic
two, leaving one equal to zero.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:09.800
Omega squared is excluded too. In general,
for a nontrivial root eta of odd order m,

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.810
eta minus one times its
geometric sum is zero.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.740
Cancel eta minus one before reduction.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:19.810
Reducing the sum gives
an odd number of ones,

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.940
again a contradiction. Therefore
R and W equal one exactly.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:29.760
Unpack this equality. The
ratios d over c and g over

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.580
c each keep their own value between
inputs. They need not equal each other.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:38.720
In our schematic, these
ratios are minus one and i.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:42.770
Set lambda equal to c
of omega over c of one.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:46.060
The same lambda multiplies c, d, and g.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:48.980
Our illustrated lambda is omega:

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:52.000
counterclockwise one
hundred twenty degrees.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.620
Delta of c measures the reverse rotation.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:59.810
Recall E of P: d of P minus
c of P, divided by two,

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:03.460
plus g of P minus c of
P, divided by one plus i.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.980
This fixed linear combination
inherits the common factor.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:12.080
Substitute lambda times each value
at one, then factor lambda out.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.040
E of omega equals lambda E of one.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:20.180
The schematic has E of one equal
to negative three plus three i,

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:21.560
outside the circle.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:25.080
Divide E of omega by c of omega.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:29.330
The common lambda cancels,
leaving E of one over c of one.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:33.000
Both schematic normalized
values are negative one plus i.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.500
This cancellation is an exact
equality before modular reduction.

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:42.260
Separately, the hypothetical
Hadamard construction requires E

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:45.040
congruent to the nine
term all ones polynomial.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:51.110
Its evaluations require E of one to have
residue one and E of omega residue zero.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:55.500
These are requirements, not residues
computed from our schematic arrows.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.840
Lambda remains invertible after
reduction, so it cannot carry a

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:04.640
nonzero residue to zero. Node seven
writes the contradiction algebraically.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:10.170
For general odd u, the same
rigidity gives R and W equal to one.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:13.940
With several distinct prime divisors,
these compare alternating products.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:17.580
The two circle rotation
describes our u equals three

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:20.620
example; the deeper argument
handles those products.
